<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  IGRIS-C SDK - Fast DDS discovery configuration (example)

  Needed on the CLIENT machine only when discovery multicast does not reach the
  robot. The usual case is a robot on Wi-Fi and a client on wired Ethernet: the
  access point does not forward the 239.255.0.1 discovery group between the two
  segments, in either direction, so neither side ever announces itself to the
  other. Same subnet and a working ping do NOT imply working multicast.

  Symptom: `ros2 topic list` shows nothing from the robot, or shows only a few
  topics while the robot itself lists them all.

  Two things are required, and NEITHER alone is enough:

    1. initialPeersList - the robot's address, so discovery can go out unicast
       instead of relying on the multicast that is being dropped.
    2. maxInitialPeersRange - Fast DDS probes only participant ids 0-3 by
       default. A robot runs more processes than that, so unicast discovery
       without this finds whichever handful of participants happen to hold the
       low ids, and which ones those are changes on every restart. Raising it
       to 32 covers every participant the robot starts.

  Note there is no <port> in the locator. Fast DDS derives the well-known
  discovery port from ROS_DOMAIN_ID at run time, so this file works unchanged
  on any domain. The ONLY environment-specific value is the address below.

  Usage (both variables are required):

      export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=$PWD/fastdds_peers.xml
      export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=<robot's igris_c.network.domain_id>
      ros2 daemon stop        # see below
      ros2 topic list

  `ros2 daemon stop` is not optional. A daemon already running without this
  profile has cached its discovery results, and CLI commands ask that daemon -
  so the profile appears to do nothing until the daemon is restarted.

  The variable name is FASTRTPS_, not FASTDDS_: Fast DDS was formerly Fast RTPS
  and kept the old name for this variable. FASTDDS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE is not
  read at all.

  This replaces ROS_STATIC_PEERS, which sets the address but cannot raise the
  participant-id range; unset it when using this file.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>udp_wide_peers</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
        <!-- Participant ids probed per peer address. Default is 4 (ids 0-3). -->
        <maxInitialPeersRange>32</maxInitialPeersRange>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>
    <participant profile_name="igris_c_wide_peers" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>udp_wide_peers</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- The custom descriptor above replaces the builtin UDP transport; leaving the
             builtin one enabled would keep a second UDPv4 transport at the default range. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
        <builtin>
          <initialPeersList>
            <locator>
              <udpv4>
                <!-- CHANGE this to the robot's IP address. -->
                <address>192.168.0.100</address>
              </udpv4>
            </locator>
          </initialPeersList>
        </builtin>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
